WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.920
One join that is pretty rare, but one we need to&nbsp;
know about is a self join. A self join is a join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.520
between two columns in the same table. Usually, it&nbsp;
occurs when a table has a hierarchical structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.400
such as an employee to a manager or a product&nbsp;
with a parent product. In the BikeRepair database,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.160
I have added an Employees table. In running&nbsp;
a SELECT star (SELECT*) statement, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:28.640
select everything from this table, we&nbsp;
see that most employees have a manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:35.120
with a corresponding EmployeeID. Our goal is to&nbsp;
display the last names of both the employee and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:42.000
the manager. On closer inspection, two employees&nbsp;
should have Bridges as the manager, and the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.720
should have Handel as the manager. Let's take a&nbsp;
look at the query that has been built using a self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:56.400
join. I want to first focus on the line with the&nbsp;
join, as the same table is being joined to itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:04.080
However, each table is being given an alias name,&nbsp;
with e for employees and m for what will represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.640
managers. Then in the join itself, each employee&nbsp;
manager is matching up with an employee ID. Above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:17.840
the self join, we see the EmployeeID and LastName&nbsp;
being listed with a column alias of employee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:25.840
and the representation of the manager's last&nbsp;
name is listed as manager. I will execute this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.920
and sure enough, we see that two employees&nbsp;
have Bridges as the manager and two have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.960
Handel as the manager. Before we move on to&nbsp;
the next topic, here is a question for you:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.440
What happened to the employee with no manager? The&nbsp;
answer lies in the join. Because this is an inner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.440
join, only employees with managers will appear.&nbsp;
To get the employee without a manager to display,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:57.600
what kind of join does this need to be? If you&nbsp;
said left join, you are correct as we want to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:07.840
all employees, including those without managers.&nbsp;
I will change the join and execute this statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.320
We now see all five employees.

